"AArch64 pair-atomic"
(* Contains material from herdtools7's bundled cat models (CeCILL-B); see LICENSE-herdtools7.txt. *)

(* Uses herd7's aarch64.cat (copyright Arm Ltd) by `include' only and contains
   none of its text; this file's own content is ours. *)

(*
 * The stock Armv8 application-level model, plus TWO axioms giving CASP
 * genuine pair (cross-location) atomicity.
 *
 * Status: these are OUR axioms, not Arm's.  They are validated by probe, not
 * transcribed from an authoritative source -- unlike cats/x86tso-mixed-rmw.cat,
 * whose repair was copied from aarch64's own `aob'.  Treat them accordingly.
 * The second one, however, is a *reinstatement* of Arm's own mechanism rather
 * than an invention; see "THE SECOND AXIOM" below.
 *
 *
 * WHY THIS IS NEEDED
 *
 * herd7 7.58 decomposes a CASP into independent per-word events.
 * AArch64Sem.ml's `casp' builds the success path as
 *
 *   read_mem  a = rmw_amo_read  ... a >>| (add_size a sz >>= rmw_amo_read  ...)
 *   write_mem a = rmw_amo_write ... a v1 >>| (add_size a sz >>= rmw_amo_write ...)
 *
 * and `>>|' is parallel composition -- nothing couples the two locations.
 * Stock aarch64.cat's atomicity axiom (`atomic': no coherence-later write may
 * come between an rmw's read and its write, stated with fr and co)
 *
 * is per-location by construction, since fr and co both relate same-location
 * events.  So a *successful* CASP may partially commit.  Witness, from
 * probe H-diag.litmus under -variant mixed (the probes were scratch and are
 * not checked in):
 *
 *   0:X2=0; 0:X3=0; 1:X2=1; 1:X3=0; x[0]=1; x[1]=0;
 *
 * P0 read (0,0), matching its expected pair, so P0 succeeded -- yet only
 * x[0]=1 landed.  P1 read the torn intermediate (1,0).
 *
 *
 * WHAT DOES NOT WORK
 *
 * The obvious generalisation of the adjacency axiom,
 *
 *   let prmw = same-instr & (R * W)
 *   empty prmw & (fre; coe)
 *
 * collapses straight back to per-location.  fre runs from a read to writes at
 * *its own* location and coe likewise, so the composition silently forces
 * loc(w) = loc(r) and you have re-derived the axiom that is already there.
 * Cross-location atomicity is not an adjacency property.
 *
 *
 * THE FIRST AXIOM: pair-atomic
 *
 * The tear has a cycle shape.  Writing r1,r2 for the reads of one pair
 * instruction and w1,w2 for the writes of another:
 *
 *   r1 --same-instr--> r2 --fr--> w2 --same-instr--> w1 --rf--> r1
 *
 * One read of the pair saw the other instruction's write, while the other
 * read of the same pair saw a state before that instruction's corresponding
 * write.  That is exactly "the two reads did not come from a consistent
 * snapshot", and forbidding it is one irreflexivity constraint.
 *
 * `same-instr' is a built-in (machModelChecker.ml:449) relating *all* events
 * of any multi-event instruction -- including the two reads of an LDP and the
 * two writes of an STP.  Constraining both sides with it would forbid
 * LDP/STP tearing, i.e. impose FEAT_LSE2 on baseline hardware.  That would be
 * wrong.
 *
 * So the *write* side is restricted to range(rmw): the cycle is only forbidden
 * when the instruction being observed is a genuine read-modify-write.  This
 * says "nobody may observe a pair RMW's write torn", which is right, while
 * leaving plain STP non-atomic, which is also right.
 *
 * cats/aarch64-lse2.cat is that same model with exactly this restriction
 * dropped -- one token, `range(rmw) * range(rmw)' becomes `W * W' -- because
 * FEAT_LSE2 is precisely the hardware feature that makes the paragraph above
 * no longer true.  It is a separate file, checked against a separate
 * directory, so that this file continues to say what baseline Armv8 permits.
 *
 * The *read* side is deliberately NOT restricted.  A failing CASP's events are
 * not in `rmw' at all, and a failing CASP must still read its pair atomically
 * (probe C-read-tear).  Restricting the read side to RMW events was the first
 * attempt and it silently did nothing.
 *
 *
 * THE SECOND AXIOM: pair-external
 *
 * pair-atomic constrains only observers that read the pair with ONE
 * instruction.  A scratch probe asked what happens when the observer uses two
 * separate, ordered loads -- probe K:
 *
 *   P0: CASP X2,X3,X4,X5,[X0]   | P1: LDR X2,[X0,#8] ; DMB SY ; LDR X3,[X0]
 *
 * P1 sees the new second word and then, after a full barrier, the OLD first
 * word.  `same-instr' does not relate two different LDRs, so pair-atomic says
 * nothing, and both stock herd7 and this model reported `Sometimes'.  Probe K2
 * is the same shape with the words swapped, and also reported `Sometimes'.
 *
 * Arm already has the mechanism for this and herd7 already computes it:
 * `sca-class', the single-copy-atomicity class, which aarch64.cat threads
 * through `obs' by letting an explicit or tag observation continue by one
 * optional single-copy-atomicity step.  Once two writes are in one class,
 * observing either one puts you after both, and the existing `irreflexive ob'
 * forbids K and K2 with no new axiom at all.
 *
 * Measured with a scratch cat, sca-probe.cat, on probe K (not checked in):
 *
 *   Flag sca-nontrivial    (sca-class \ id)                        non-empty
 *   Flag pairw-offdiag     (same-instr & range(rmw)^2) \ id        non-empty
 *   Flag pairw-not-sca     ... \ sca-class                         non-empty
 *
 * The first says herd7 does build single-copy-atomicity classes here.  The
 * third says a CASP's two writes are not in one -- which is the bug, stated
 * exactly.  (`-variant S128' does not change this.)
 *
 * So the repair is to widen the class and re-run Arm's own external-visibility
 * requirement over it.  `ob2' below is the transitive closure of aarch64.cat's
 * `hw-reqs' and `obs' plus the two observation relations followed by an `sca2'
 * step.  Since ob2 includes ob, `irreflexive ob2' implies stock's
 * `irreflexive ob'; the stock axiom is left in place anyway.
 *
 *
 * TRAP: `catdep'
 *
 * The `catdep' option below is load-bearing and must be repeated here.  It is
 * declared in stock aarch64.cat and is NOT inherited through `include'.
 * Without it, herd7 stops using the cat file's dependency computation and
 * MP+dmb.st+addr, MP+dmb.st+ctrlisb, LB+ctrl and LB+data all silently flip
 * from forbidden to allowed.  A bare `include "aarch64.cat"' wrapper with no
 * axiom at all reproduces that, which is how it was diagnosed.
 *
 *
 * VALIDATION
 *
 * Measured when `pair-external` was added.  Every line of it was unchanged
 * from before.  The counts below are what was on disk *that day*; both
 * directories have grown since, and the current numbers are the ones
 * ./check-herd.sh prints, not these:
 *
 * - all 22 AArch64 tests then in litmus/aarch64/ were bit-identical to stock
 *   (verdict and state count), so the axioms are conservative over the harness.
 *   Re-measured at 25 tests when cats/aarch64-lse2.cat was added: still
 *   bit-identical, both for this file and for that one
 * - all 4 pair tests that existed then were bit-identical to this file's
 *   previous, one-axiom version.  `CASPOrderedRead` and
 *   `CASPOrderedReadSwapped` were added afterwards, and litmus/aarch64-pair/
 *   holds 12<!--@herd-pair--> today; all nine are checked on every run of
 *   ./check-herd.sh, which is the standing gate this block is only the origin
 *   story for
 * - the nine CASP probes were bit-identical to the previous version, and every
 *   contended one still collapsed to exactly two states, "P0 wins" or "P1
 *   wins".  Those probes were scratch and are not checked in
 * - I-ldp-stp.litmus reports Sometimes under BOTH this model and stock, so
 *   FEAT_LSE2 has not been imposed by accident.  That probe is no longer
 *   scratch: it lives in litmus/aarch64-lse2/, where the gate re-runs it under
 *   the model that *does* impose FEAT_LSE2 and requires Never.  Only that
 *   direction is gated -- the Sometimes reported here is still a one-off
 *   measurement, because litmus/aarch64-pair/ is generated from WeakMem.lean
 *   and a hand-written probe cannot be added to it
 * - J-plain-write-tear.litmus stays Sometimes: two ordinary STRs to the two
 *   words are still allowed to be seen half-done, so the write side really is
 *   still restricted to RMWs
 * - K and K2 flip from Sometimes to Never, which is the point
 *
 * The full probe table was scratch and is not checked in.
 *)

catdep

include "aarch64.cat"

(* Any two events of one instruction. *)
let pairAny = same-instr

(* Two writes of one read-modify-write instruction. *)
let pairRMWW = same-instr & (range(rmw) * range(rmw))

irreflexive (pairAny ; fr ; pairRMWW ; rf) as pair-atomic

(* A pair RMW's two writes are one single-copy-atomic access.  `sca2' widens
   aarch64.cat's `sca-class' by them; `ob2' is aarch64.cat's ordered-before,
   referenced by name (`hw-reqs', `obs', `Exp-obs', `Tag-obs'), with the
   explicit and tag observations also allowed to continue by an `sca2' step.
   As sca-class <= sca2, this is the old obs2/ob2 pair, with `+' for the
   recursive `ob2 ; ob2'. *)
let sca2 = (sca-class | pairRMWW)+

let ob2 = (hw-reqs | obs | Exp-obs ; sca2 | Tag-obs ; sca2)+

irreflexive ob2 as pair-external
